  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather is expected through Thursday \nwith climbing temperatures.\n\nMoisture associated with the departing Nor'Easter remains over the \nMid-Atlantic through this afternoon, yielding increased cloud cover \nand perhaps some light drizzle/rain showers. Upper level ridging \nslowly builds overhead beginning tonight with surface high pressure \nremaining overhead through Thursday.  \n\nWith upper level ridging and surface high pressure overhead, dry \nconditions and warming temperatures are expected through the end of \nthe week. High temperatures today will rise into the 60s and 70s \nwith highs by Thursday rising in the 90s for portions of the area. \nOn Friday, surface high pressure shifts offshore as a cold front \napproaches from the northwest. Dry conditions and warming \ntemperatures continue for most with the exception of afternoon rain \nshowers in the western portions of the Alleghenies.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A stalling cold front and wave of low pressure \nmay affect the area this weekend.\n\nUpper troughing shears out toward the Great Lakes, eventually \nbecoming absorbed in a larger cyclonic gyre near Hudson Bay this \nweekend. At the surface, this pattern sends a cold front southward \ninto Virginia. There is some lingering uncertainty as to where the \nfront stalls, with a sharp temperature gradient across the frontal \nzone (60s on the north/cool side, 80s on the south/warm side). The \ntemperature forecast for Saturday may be subject to large changes of \n10 or more degrees as a result, with the current forecast splitting \nthe difference and calling for widespread 60s and 70s given at least \nsome broad consensus that the front ends up to the south placing the \nMid-Atlantic on its cooler and cloudier side. Shower activity may \naccompany the stalling front, with uncertainty in coverage (though \nanything severe or particularly heavy seems unlikely at this point).\n\nA wave of low pressure, infused with tropical moisture from the \nremnants of multiple East Pacific systems, treks toward the OH/TN \nValleys Saturday into Saturday night. The track of the low beyond \nthis point will depend on (1) where exactly the stalled front is, \nand (2) the strength of a transient blocking high pressure to the \nnorth. Rain or showers become increasingly likely for much of the \nregion Saturday night into Sunday, with a soaking rain possible if \nthe wave of low pressure is a bit more organized/closer to the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...High pressure likely returns early next week.\n\nFollowing the weekend system, a large area of high pressure will \nlikely build from the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes to the Northeast \nand Mid-Atlantic early next week. This sets the stage for much drier \nweather and below normal temperatures for the first half of next \nweek. Daytime highs on Monday and Tuesday likely stay in the 60s \n(50s in the mountains), with overnight lows in the 40s to near 50 \n(30s in the mountains and sheltered valleys west of the Blue Ridge \nwith perhaps some frost potential in the coldest spots).",
